course: euphemisms
lesson: 35
title: Soft words for hard things: failure and mistakes and stupidity and madness
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some soft words turn a mistake into something better: a teacher may call a spilled experiment "a teachable moment", and a painter who drips paint by accident and likes it calls it "a happy accident".
explain: Others shift the blame: when a helpdesk says "user error", it means the machine is fine and the person using it got it wrong.
explain: Calling someone stupid is rude, so English has dozens of jokey ways to say it, many of them pictures of something missing: "a few sandwiches short of a picnic" means the person's head is missing a few "pieces".
explain: Australians are famous for these: "a sausage short of a barbie" or "a few kangaroos loose in the top paddock". They are still insults, even when they sound funny, so it is kind to understand them but not to use them on people.
fact: "A teachable moment" (US jargon) means a mistake to learn from.
fact: "User error" means the user made the mistake, not the machine.
fact: "A happy accident" means a mistake that turned out well.
fact: "An honest mistake" means a mistake made without bad intent.
fact: "Fell short" means failed to reach the goal.
fact: "Not the sharpest tool in the shed" is a joke meaning not very clever.
fact: "Not the brightest spark" (UK) means not very clever.
fact: "Not the brightest bulb" (US) means not very clever.
fact: "A few sandwiches short of a picnic" (UK) means stupid or a bit mad.
fact: "A brick short of a load" (Australia) means stupid or a bit mad.
fact: "Not playing with a full deck" (US) means stupid or a bit mad.
fact: "The lights are on but nobody's home" means a person is stupid or not paying attention.
fact: "A few kangaroos loose in the top paddock" (Australia) means stupid or a bit mad.
fact: "Not the full quid" (Australian slang) means not very clever or a bit mad.
fact: "A sausage short of a barbie" (Australia) means stupid or a bit mad.
fact: "A stubby short of a six-pack" (Australia) means stupid or a bit mad; a stubby is a small bottle of beer.
fact: "A bit slow" means not very clever.
fact: "Not the quickest" is a polite way of saying not very clever.
fact: "Not exactly a rocket scientist" is a joke meaning not clever.
fact: "No Einstein" is a joke meaning not clever; Albert Einstein was a very famous and very clever scientist.
exercise: The teacher spills the vinegar and says, "Well, this is a teachable moment!" What does she mean? | It is a mistake we can learn from | It is time for a test | It is the end of the lesson | It is a moment to stop teaching
exercise: The helpdesk says the printer problem was "user error". What do they mean? | The person using it made the mistake | The printer was broken | The helpdesk made the mistake when they set it up | The paper was the wrong size
exercise: What is "a happy accident" in cooking? | A mistake that turned out well | A crash that made people laugh | A dish served at a party | A burn that healed quickly
exercise: What does "it was an honest mistake" mean? | It was a mistake made without meaning harm | It was a lie told on purpose to fool someone | It was a mistake about honesty | It was not a mistake at all
exercise: What does "the team fell short of the final" mean? | The team failed to reach the final | The team was too short to play basketball | The team fell over in the final | The team won the final easily
exercise: What does "he's not the sharpest tool in the shed" mean? | He is not very clever | His tools are blunt | He keeps a messy shed | He is not good at gardening
exercise: What does "she's not the brightest spark" mean in Britain? | She is not very clever | She cannot light a fire | She does not like fireworks | She is shy and quiet
exercise: What does "he's not the brightest bulb" mean in the US? | He is not very clever | He cannot change a light bulb | He grows tulips badly | His room is too dark
exercise: What does "a few sandwiches short of a picnic" mean? | Stupid or a bit mad | Hungry at a picnic | Bad at making sandwiches | Late for a picnic
exercise: An Aussie says his neighbour is "a brick short of a load". What does he mean? | The neighbour is stupid or a bit mad | The neighbour is a builder | The neighbour needs more bricks | The neighbour's truck is empty
exercise: What does "she's not playing with a full deck" mean in the US? | She is stupid or a bit mad | She lost some playing cards | She is bad at card games | She has no boat deck
exercise: What does "the lights are on but nobody's home" mean about a person? | They are not thinking or paying attention | They left the lights on at home when they went out | They are out on holiday | They are afraid of the dark
exercise: What does "he's got a few kangaroos loose in the top paddock" mean in Australia? | He is stupid or a bit mad | He keeps kangaroos on a farm | His farm fence is broken | He has a pet kangaroo
exercise: What does "she's not the full quid" mean in Australian slang? | She is not very clever or a bit mad | She has no money left at all in her purse | She is not paid enough | She has lost her purse
exercise: What does "a sausage short of a barbie" mean? | Stupid or a bit mad | Not enough food for a barbecue | A doll with no sausage | A cook who burns sausages
exercise: What does "a stubby short of a six-pack" mean in Australia? | Stupid or a bit mad | Not very strong | Short and stubby | Missing a pack of crisps
exercise: What does "he's a bit slow" usually mean about a pupil? | He is not very clever | He walks slowly | He runs slower than others | He is always late
exercise: The coach says, "He's not the quickest, bless him." What is the coach politely saying? | He is not very clever | He is a slow runner | He is very young | He arrives late every day
exercise: What does "she's not exactly a rocket scientist" mean? | She is not very clever | She does not build rockets | She works at a different job | She is scared of space
exercise: What does "he's no Einstein" mean? | He is not very clever | He is not called Albert | He is not a German | He has short hair
test: "printer won't work, helpdesk says user error. rude!!" What is the helpdesk saying? | The person using it got it wrong | The printer is broken | The helpdesk is at fault for the trouble | The ink has run out
test: "The new bridge fell short of what was promised." What does this say about the bridge? | It was not as good as promised | It was too short to cross the river | It fell down | It was finished early
test: "I spilled blue ink on my drawing and it looked like the sea — a happy accident!" How did the spill turn out? | Well | Badly | It was planned | Nobody saw it
test: Gossip: "Her new boyfriend? Lovely, but not the sharpest tool in the shed." What is said about him? | He is not very clever | He is a gardener | He has no tools | He is not kind
test: "U put the milk in the oven?? lights are on but nobody's home, mate." What is the speaker saying? | You were not thinking at all | The lights in the house are on | Nobody is at home | The oven is broken
test: An Australian uncle: "That bloke who parked on the beach? Few kangaroos loose in the top paddock." What does he think of the man? | He is a bit mad | He is a farmer | He is lost | He is very brave
test: "Called it an honest mistake, but he kept the change three times." Why might the speaker doubt it? | Doing it three times looks like it was on purpose | Honest mistakes happen three times | Change is always kept | He gave the money back
test: Leo says: "'He's no Einstein' means he's not famous, right?" Is Leo right? | No, it means he is not very clever | Yes, it means he is not famous | Yes, it means he has no hair | No, it means he is not old
test: A teacher to a parent: "Max is a bit slow with fractions." What is the teacher saying gently? | Max finds fractions hard | Max writes fractions slowly but well | Max is the best at fractions | Max is often late for maths
test: "shut up, you're a sausage short of a barbie, it's Tuesday not Friday" What does the speaker think of the friend? | The friend is being stupid | The friend is hungry | The friend forgot the barbecue | The friend is a good cook
test: The science teacher drops the egg: "Ha! A teachable moment, everyone — what went wrong?" What is she doing? | Turning her mistake into a lesson | Starting a test | Blaming a pupil | Ending the lesson early
test: A boss about a new worker: "Not exactly a rocket scientist, but he works hard." What is said? | He is not very clever but works hard | He is a scientist who works hard | He is clever and lazy | He builds rockets at weekends
exam: Whereas a technical support department attributes a malfunction to "user error", identify the party held responsible thereby. | The person operating the equipment | The manufacturer of the equipment | The technical support department itself | No party whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the colloquial Australian expression "a sausage short of a barbie" when applied to a person. | The person is foolish or somewhat irrational | The person has insufficient provisions for a barbecue | The person is employed as a butcher | The person is an accomplished cook
exam: It is given that an outcome is described as "a happy accident". Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the outcome? | An unintended error produced a favourable result | A collision occurred in which nobody was injured | A deliberate plan was executed successfully | An unfortunate event occasioned general amusement
exam: Determine the meaning of the expression "fell short" as employed in respect of a team's attempt to reach a final. | The team failed to attain its objective | The team was of insufficient physical stature | The team fell to the ground during the final | The team attained its objective with ease
